---
import { Image, getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import CallButton from '../components/CallButton.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
import { IMG } from '../data/images';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How long does water damage restoration take in Baytown?',
    a: 'Drying alone usually takes several days, and repairs add more time after that. A small clean-water leak caught early sits at the short end, while wet wall cavities, hardwood or water that sat over a weekend take longer. The job is finished when moisture readings match the dry parts of the house.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How fast can mold grow after water damage?',
    a: 'Mold can start within a day or two. The <a href="https://www.epa.gov/mold/brief-guide-mold-moisture-and-your-home" rel="noopener">EPA</a> says materials dried within 24 to 48 hours usually will not grow mold, and the Texas Department of Insurance notes growth can begin within a day of a leak.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can wet carpet and pad be saved?',
    a: 'Carpet often can be saved after a clean-water leak, but the pad usually cannot. Carpet soaked by a supply line break and dried quickly is commonly relaid, while the pad holds water and is cheap to replace. Carpet and pad soaked by floodwater or sewage are normally thrown out.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is water damage from a burst pipe covered by insurance?',
    a: 'Often yes, because a burst pipe is the kind of sudden, accidental loss most home policies cover. The <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/tips/when-are-water-damage-and-mold-covered-by-insurance.html" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> uses burst pipes as an example of covered damage, and says gradual leaks are usually excluded. Report the claim early and keep the broken part.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What should I do while waiting for the restoration crew?',
    a: 'Keep the water off, stay away from wet electrical outlets, and document everything. Photograph every wet room, lift furniture off wet floors and move electronics and papers somewhere dry.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it safe to stay in the house during drying?',
    a: 'Usually yes after a clean-water leak, but not after sewage, floodwater, heavy mold or a fire. Drying equipment is loud and warms the room, so many families stay in the unaffected rooms. When the water was contaminated or mold is being removed, keep people out of the area, especially anyone with asthma.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between water mitigation and restoration?',
    a: 'Mitigation stops the damage from spreading, and restoration puts the house back together. Mitigation covers extraction, removing materials that cannot be saved and drying. Restoration is the repair work that follows, such as new drywall, paint and flooring.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I know the walls are completely dry?',
    a: 'Moisture readings tell you, not touch or appearance. Drywall can feel dry while the framing behind it is still wet. A technician compares readings with the same material in a dry part of the house, and drying is complete when they match.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should I call about water damage?',
    a: `Call as soon as you find it, because the phone line is answered day and night. Shut off the water first if you can do it safely, then call the same day so drying can start inside the 24 to 48 hour window.`,
  },
];

const heroOpt = await getImage({ src: IMG.hero.src, width: 900, format: 'webp', quality: 62 });
const heroSm = await getImage({ src: IMG.hero.src, width: 560, format: 'webp', quality: 60 });
const heroSrcset = `${heroSm.src} 560w, ${heroOpt.src} 900w`;
const heroSizes = '(max-width: 980px) calc(100vw - 64px), 42vw';

const cardSizes = '(max-width: 640px) 100vw, (max-width: 980px) 50vw, 30vw';
---
<Base
  title="24/7 Water Damage & Fire Restoration Baytown TX | Help Line"
  description="Water damage restoration in Baytown, TX: extraction, drying, mold and fire cleanup, costs, insurance and a local help line to get a crew scheduled."
  faqs={faqs}
  preloadImage={{ src: heroOpt.src, srcset: heroSrcset, sizes: heroSizes }}
>
  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero__text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Baytown, TX / Water and fire damage help</p>
        <h1>Water Damage and Fire <em>Restoration</em> in Baytown, TX</h1>
        <p class="hero__tags">Water &middot; Fire &middot; Smoke &middot; Storm damage</p>
        <p class="hero__lead">
          Water damage restoration in Baytown, TX, with fast drying and safe cleanup after leaks, floods, fires and smoke. Call any time, day or night, to get a restoration crew scheduled.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <CallButton variant="btn--lg" label="Call 24/7: {phone}" location="hero" />
          <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--lg" href="#services">See services</a>
        </div>
        <ul class="hero__bullets">
          <li><strong>Day or night</strong> help line</li>
          <li><strong>Free</strong> to call</li>
          <li><strong>No obligation</strong> to hire</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <figure class="frame">
        <img
          src={heroOpt.src}
          srcset={heroSrcset}
          sizes={heroSizes}
          width={heroOpt.attributes.width}
          height={heroOpt.attributes.height}
          alt={IMG.hero.alt}
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption><span><b>Water damage</b> {IMG.hero.short}</span><span>{IMG.hero.credit}</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FACTS BAND -->
  <section class="trust" aria-label="Key facts">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="trust__card">
        <ul class="trust__items">
          <li><Icon name="clock" /><div><strong>24/7 phone line</strong><span>Calls answered day and night</span></div></li>
          <li><Icon name="phone" /><div><strong>Free to call</strong><span>No charge for the call</span></div></li>
          <li><Icon name="pin" /><div><strong>3 Baytown ZIP codes</strong><span>{SITE.zips.join(', ')}</span></div></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 1. WHAT IT COVERS -->
  <section class="section" id="what-it-covers">
    <div class="wrap split-text">
      <div class="home-text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Water damage</p>
        <h2>Water Damage Restoration in Baytown, TX: <em>What It Covers</em></h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Water damage restoration in Baytown, TX is the work of removing water from a home, drying the structure and contents, and returning the house to its condition before the leak or flood. It starts with stopping the source and ends when moisture readings are back to normal and repairs are done.
        </p>
        <p>A complete job usually includes six parts:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Inspection.</strong> Finding the source, confirming it has stopped, and mapping where the water traveled.</li>
          <li><strong>Water extraction.</strong> Pulling standing water out of floors, carpet and pad with powerful extractors and pumps.</li>
          <li><strong>Drying.</strong> Air movers push dry air across wet surfaces so moisture leaves walls, subfloors and framing.</li>
          <li><strong>Dehumidifying.</strong> Dehumidifiers remove that moisture from the air so it does not settle back into the house.</li>
          <li><strong>Cleaning and sanitizing.</strong> Surfaces the water touched are cleaned, and treated when the water was not clean.</li>
          <li><strong>Repairs coordination.</strong> Drywall, flooring, trim and paint are replaced once everything tests dry.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Each stage is explained in <a href="#process">the five-step restoration process</a> further down this page.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="takeaways takeaways--card">
        <p class="box-title">Key takeaways</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Speed matters most. The EPA advises drying wet materials within 24 to 48 hours.</li>
          <li>Extraction removes far more water than fans ever will.</li>
          <li>In Baytown humidity, a closed house with dehumidifiers dries faster than open windows.</li>
          <li>Floodwater and sewage are treated as contaminated, so more gets removed.</li>
          <li>Drying is finished when moisture readings say so, not when surfaces feel dry.</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. EMERGENCY WATER REMOVAL -->
  <section class="section bg-light" id="emergency-water-removal">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="home-text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Emergency water removal</p>
        <h2>Emergency Water Removal and <em>Water Extraction</em> in Baytown</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          The first day decides how much of your home can be saved. Emergency water removal calls are answered day and night, so you can call the moment you find the problem and get a restoration crew scheduled.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <a href="https://www.epa.gov/mold/brief-guide-mold-moisture-and-your-home" rel="noopener">EPA</a> advises drying water-damaged areas and items within 24 to 48 hours to prevent mold growth. After that window, drywall softens, wood swells and mold has time to take hold.
        </p>
        <p>Water extraction is the step that buys back that time. The equipment is very different from a shop vacuum:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Truck-mounted extractors</strong> sit outside and run long hoses in to pull large volumes of water from carpet and floors.</li>
          <li><strong>Portable extractors</strong> reach upstairs rooms and closets a truck hose cannot.</li>
          <li><strong>Submersible pumps</strong> move deep standing water, such as a flooded garage or a sunken living room.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Once standing water is gone, drying equipment takes over. Read more about <a href="/water-damage-restoration-baytown-tx/">water extraction and structural drying in Baytown</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure>
        <Image src={IMG.figs.extraction.src} alt={IMG.figs.extraction.alt} width={760} widths={[480, 760, 1100]} sizes="(max-width: 980px) 100vw, 45vw" quality={60} loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Wet vacuums pulling standing water off a concrete floor before drying equipment goes in.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. SERVICES -->
  <section class="section" id="services">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Services</p>
        <h2>Restoration Services for <em>Baytown Homes</em></h2>
        <p>Most water and fire problems fall into one of six types. Each has its own guide with more detail.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="cards cards--services">
        <li class="card">
          <Image src={IMG.water.src} alt={IMG.water.alt} width={640} widths={[400, 640, 900]} sizes={cardSizes} quality={60} loading="lazy" />
          <div class="card__body">
            <h3>Water Damage Repair and Structural Drying</h3>
            <p>Structural drying gets water out of the parts of a house you cannot see: wall cavities, subfloors and framing. Technicians pull baseboards, sometimes drill small holes low in the wall, and aim air movers into the cavity while dehumidifiers run around the clock. Water damage repair, such as new drywall and flooring, comes after the structure tests dry.</p>
            <a class="text-link" href="/water-damage-restoration-baytown-tx/">How water damage restoration works <Icon name="arrow" /></a>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <Image src={IMG.pipe.src} alt={IMG.pipe.alt} width={640} widths={[400, 640, 900]} sizes={cardSizes} quality={60} loading="lazy" />
          <div class="card__body">
            <h3>Burst Pipe and Slab Leak Water Damage</h3>
            <p>A split supply line runs at full pressure until someone closes a valve, so burst pipe water damage spreads fast. Slab leaks are slow and hidden under the concrete, often found through a high water bill. Both need a licensed plumber for the pipe and a restoration crew for the water. Shut off the main valve first.</p>
            <a class="text-link" href="/burst-pipe-water-damage-baytown/">First steps for burst pipes and slab leaks <Icon name="arrow" /></a>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <Image src={IMG.flood.src} alt={IMG.flood.alt} width={640} widths={[400, 640, 900]} sizes={cardSizes} quality={60} loading="lazy" />
          <div class="card__body">
            <h3>Flood and Storm Water Cleanup</h3>
            <p>Flood damage cleanup in Baytown follows different rules than a plumbing leak. Rising water from streets, bayous or the bay is treated as contaminated, so carpet, pad, insulation and the lower drywall it soaked are usually removed. Flood losses are usually insured under a separate flood policy, not a homeowners policy.</p>
            <a class="text-link" href="/flood-storm-water-cleanup-baytown/">Flood cleanup after storms and heavy rain <Icon name="arrow" /></a>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <Image src={IMG.figs.floodVac.src} alt={IMG.figs.floodVac.alt} width={640} widths={[400, 640, 900]} sizes={cardSizes} quality={60} loading="lazy" />
          <div class="card__body">
            <h3>Sewage Backup Cleanup (Category 3 Water)</h3>
            <p>Sewage backup cleanup is not a do-it-yourself job. Water from a backed-up drain or toilet carries bacteria and other pathogens, and the <a href="https://www.epa.gov/mold/mold-cleanup-your-home" rel="noopener">EPA</a> advises calling a professional with experience in contaminated water. Crews wear protective gear, remove soaked porous materials, clean and disinfect what remains, and only then start drying. Keep children and pets out of the area.</p>
            <a class="text-link" href="#water-categories">What Category 3 water means <Icon name="arrow" /></a>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <Image src={IMG.mold.src} alt={IMG.mold.alt} width={640} widths={[400, 640, 900]} sizes={cardSizes} quality={60} loading="lazy" />
          <div class="card__body">
            <h3>Mold Remediation After Water Damage</h3>
            <p>Mold remediation after water damage is regulated in Texas. The Texas Department of Licensing and Regulation licenses mold assessors and remediators, and most professional work on 25 contiguous square feet or more of visible mold requires a license. Fast drying is still the cheapest form of mold removal in Baytown, because it stops growth before it starts.</p>
            <a class="text-link" href="/mold-after-water-damage-baytown/">Mold after water damage and Texas licensing <Icon name="arrow" /></a>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <Image src={IMG.fire.src} alt={IMG.fire.alt} width={640} widths={[400, 640, 900]} sizes={cardSizes} quality={60} loading="lazy" />
          <div class="card__body">
            <h3>Fire, Smoke and Soot Damage Restoration</h3>
            <p>Fire damage reaches far past the burned area. Smoke and soot cleanup covers rooms the flames never touched, and firefighting water needs drying like any other leak. Soot is acidic and keeps staining surfaces, so cleaning should start soon after the fire department clears the house.</p>
            <a class="text-link" href="/fire-smoke-damage-restoration-baytown-tx/">Smoke and soot cleanup in Baytown <Icon name="arrow" /></a>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. FIRE AND SMOKE -->
  <section class="section bg-dark" id="fire-damage">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <figure>
        <Image src={IMG.hiring.src} alt={IMG.hiring.alt} width={760} widths={[480, 760, 1100]} sizes="(max-width: 980px) 100vw, 45vw" quality={60} loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Plastic containment and an air scrubber keep soot from spreading to clean rooms during fire cleanup.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="home-text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Fire and smoke damage</p>
        <h2>Fire and Smoke Damage <em>Restoration in Baytown</em></h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Fire damage restoration in Baytown starts only after the fire department says the house is safe to enter. The work then follows a set order.
        </p>
        <ol class="plain-steps">
          <li><strong>Safety clearance.</strong> The <a href="https://www.redcross.org/get-help/how-to-prepare-for-emergencies/types-of-emergencies/fire/home-fire-recovery.html" rel="noopener">American Red Cross</a> advises staying out until local fire authorities say it is safe to re-enter. Leave utilities off until a professional restores them.</li>
          <li><strong>Board-up and tarping.</strong> Broken windows and roof openings are covered so rain and trespassers stay out.</li>
          <li><strong>Water removal.</strong> Firefighting water soaks carpet, subfloors and the rooms below, and it needs fast drying.</li>
          <li><strong>Soot and smoke residue cleanup.</strong> Loose soot is removed dry first. Wiping it with a wet rag tends to smear it into paint.</li>
          <li><strong>Odor removal.</strong> Smoke odor lives in drywall, insulation, fabric and ductwork, so those materials are cleaned or replaced.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <CallButton offer="fire" label="Baytown, TX help line: {phone}" location="home-fire" />
        </div>
        <p class="after-btn">Read the full guide to <a href="/fire-smoke-damage-restoration-baytown-tx/">fire damage restoration in Baytown</a>, including what to clean yourself and what to leave alone.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. SIGNS -->
  <section class="section" id="signs">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Signs to watch for</p>
        <h2>Signs You Need Professional <em>Water Damage Cleanup</em></h2>
        <p>Hidden water often does more harm than the puddle you can see. Any of these signs is worth a moisture check.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li><strong>Soft or stained drywall.</strong> Drywall that gives under your thumb or shows a tide line has absorbed water from behind.</li>
        <li><strong>A sagging ceiling.</strong> A bulge means water is pooling above it. Keep everyone out from under it.</li>
        <li><strong>Warped or cupping floors.</strong> Hardwood boards that rise at the edges are absorbing moisture from below.</li>
        <li><strong>A musty smell.</strong> An earthy odor, often strongest when the air conditioner starts, can point to hidden mold.</li>
        <li><strong>Rising indoor humidity.</strong> Foggy windows or a dehumidifier that fills fast can mean water is evaporating somewhere inside.</li>
        <li><strong>Peeling or bubbling paint.</strong> Paint lifts when moisture pushes out from the wall underneath.</li>
        <li><strong>Stains that spread after rain.</strong> A ceiling stain that grows after each storm points to a roof or flashing leak.</li>
      </ul>
      <ul class="gallery gallery--compact">
        {IMG.grid.slice(0, 3).map((g) => (
          <li>
            <figure>
              <Image src={g.src} alt={g.alt} width={600} widths={[360, 600, 900]} sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 30vw" quality={58} loading="lazy" />
              <figcaption><b>{g.title}</b>{g.caption}{g.credit !== 'Illustration' && <small>Photo: {g.credit}</small>}</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="after-btn">If mold is already visible, read about <a href="/mold-after-water-damage-baytown/">mold remediation after water damage</a> before you start scrubbing.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. PROCESS -->
  <section class="section bg-light" id="process">
    <div class="wrap split split--top">
      <div class="home-text">
        <p class="eyebrow">The process</p>
        <h2>How the Water Damage <em>Restoration Process</em> Works</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          A sound restoration job follows the same five steps every time. Reputable companies follow the <a href="https://iicrc.org/s500/" rel="noopener">ANSI/IICRC S500 standard</a> for professional water damage restoration, so it is a fair question to ask whoever comes out.
        </p>
        <ol class="steps steps--light">
          <li>
            <h3>Inspection and Moisture Mapping</h3>
            <p>The technician finds the source and confirms it is stopped. Then a moisture meter, and sometimes a thermal camera, traces how far the water spread. Water moves under flooring and up drywall, so the wet area is usually larger than the stain.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Water Extraction</h3>
            <p>Extractors and pumps remove standing water and the water held in carpet. Carpet may be lifted so the pad can be pulled and the floor underneath dried.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Drying and Dehumidification</h3>
            <p>Air movers create airflow across wet surfaces, and dehumidifiers pull the moisture out of the room air. In humid Baytown weather the house is usually kept closed so the equipment can control the air. Readings should be taken and logged daily, and equipment moved as areas dry.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Cleaning, Sanitizing and Odor Control</h3>
            <p>Every surface the water touched is cleaned. After gray or black water, surfaces are also disinfected, and air scrubbers may run to capture dust and spores.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Repairs and Rebuild Coordination</h3>
            <p>Once readings confirm the structure is dry, removed drywall, insulation, flooring and trim are replaced. Some companies do the rebuild and some hand it to a general contractor, so ask which applies. More detail is in the <a href="/water-damage-restoration-baytown-tx/">water damage repair guide for Baytown homes</a>.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <figure class="sticky-fig">
        <Image src={IMG.figs.waterFloor.src} alt={IMG.figs.waterFloor.alt} width={760} widths={[480, 760, 1100]} sizes="(max-width: 980px) 100vw, 40vw" quality={60} loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Moisture readings decide when drying is finished, not how a floor looks.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. CATEGORIES AND CLASSES -->
  <section class="section" id="water-categories">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head section-head--wide">
        <p class="eyebrow">Know your water</p>
        <h2>Water Categories and <em>Damage Classes</em> Explained</h2>
        <p>Crews label each job with two terms from the IICRC S500 standard: the category (how dirty the water is) and the class (how much water there is).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-grid">
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="data-table">
            <caption>Water categories (contamination)</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">What it means</th><th scope="col">Typical Baytown example</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Category 1, clean</td><td>From a sanitary source with little risk</td><td>Split supply line, leaking water heater tank</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Category 2, gray</td><td>Contaminated enough to cause illness</td><td>Washing machine or dishwasher overflow</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Category 3, black</td><td>Grossly contaminated, may carry pathogens</td><td>Sewage backup, street floodwater, storm surge</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="data-table">
            <caption>Damage classes (amount of water and drying difficulty)</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Class</th><th scope="col">What it usually looks like</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Class 1</td><td>A small area, with little water absorbed into materials</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Class 2</td><td>A whole room, with wet carpet and pad and water wicking up the walls</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Class 3</td><td>The most water, often from above, soaking ceilings, walls and insulation</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Class 4</td><td>Water held in dense materials like hardwood, plaster, brick or concrete that need specialty drying</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="home-text">
        <p>
          The labels change the plan. Category 1 water caught early often lets crews dry carpet and drywall in place. Category 3 water means wet porous materials are removed, workers wear protective gear and everything left is disinfected before drying. Clean water turns gray or black if it sits, so the category can get worse with time. Rising water from a storm is a common Category 3 case, covered in <a href="/flood-storm-water-cleanup-baytown/">storm water cleanup in Baytown</a>. A higher class means more equipment, more drying days and often more demolition. The current S500 edition refines these definitions, so ask which category and class your job was given and why.
        </p>
        <p class="source-note">Sources: <a href="https://iicrc.org/s500/" rel="noopener">IICRC S500 Standard for Professional Water Damage Restoration</a>; <a href="https://www.epa.gov/mold/mold-cleanup-your-home" rel="noopener">EPA, Mold Cleanup in Your Home</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. CAUSES -->
  <section class="section bg-light" id="causes">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head section-head--wide">
        <p class="eyebrow">Local risks</p>
        <h2>Common Causes of <em>Water Damage in Baytown</em> Homes</h2>
        <p>Baytown is bordered by water on three sides, with Galveston Bay to the south and west and Cedar Bayou to the east. Flat ground and a low shoreline make flooding a recurring problem here, but most water damage still starts inside the house.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cause-grid">
        <div>
          <p><strong class="cause-title">Hurricanes and tropical storms</strong>Hurricane season runs June through November. During Hurricane Harvey, the National Weather Service recorded a storm total of <a href="https://www.weather.gov/hgx/hurricaneharvey" rel="noopener">42.90 inches at its "2 NW Baytown" report site</a> from August 25 to 31, 2017.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p><strong class="cause-title">Heavy rain and street flooding</strong>A slow line of thunderstorms can overwhelm street drainage without any named storm. Parts of the shoreline also sank as groundwater was pumped out over decades.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p><strong class="cause-title">Hard freezes and burst pipes</strong>Freezes are rare but costly. In February 2021 the National Weather Service recorded 13&deg;F in Houston and reported widespread power outages and broken pipes across the region.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p><strong class="cause-title">Slab leaks</strong>Many homes here sit on concrete slabs with water lines below. A slab leak can soak flooring for weeks before anyone notices.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p><strong class="cause-title">Water heaters and appliances</strong>Tank water heaters rust through at the bottom, and washer hoses and ice maker lines fail without warning.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p><strong class="cause-title">Roof leaks and AC drain lines</strong>Wind-driven rain gets past worn flashing. In the humid summer, a clogged AC drain line or rusted pan can stain the ceiling below.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="source-note">Sources: <a href="https://www.weather.gov/hgx/hurricaneharvey" rel="noopener">NWS, Hurricane Harvey</a>; <a href="https://www.weather.gov/hgx/2021ValentineStorm" rel="noopener">NWS, February 2021 winter outbreak</a>. More on storm cleanup in <a href="/flood-storm-water-cleanup-baytown/">flood and storm water cleanup in Baytown</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. COST AND INSURANCE -->
  <section class="section" id="cost">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="home-text home-text--center">
        <p class="eyebrow">Cost</p>
        <h2>What Affects the Cost of <em>Water Damage Repair</em> in Baytown</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          No honest source can quote a price before an inspection. Cost depends on a handful of factors, and knowing them helps you read an estimate.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Size of the affected area.</strong> More wet area means more equipment and labor.</li>
          <li><strong>Water category.</strong> Contaminated water means protective gear, disinfection and more materials removed.</li>
          <li><strong>How long the water sat.</strong> Every extra day spreads the water and raises the mold risk.</li>
          <li><strong>Materials affected.</strong> Hardwood, cabinets and plaster take longer to dry or cost more to replace than carpet pad.</li>
          <li><strong>Equipment and drying days.</strong> Air movers and dehumidifiers are usually billed per unit per day.</li>
          <li><strong>Mold.</strong> Mold beyond the Texas exemption size adds licensed assessment and remediation.</li>
          <li><strong>Repairs.</strong> Drywall, paint, flooring and trim are often priced separately from drying.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Ask for a written, itemized estimate, and ask how changes will be approved if the job grows.</p>

        <h3>Does Homeowners Insurance Cover Water Damage?</h3>
        <p>
          Often, if the damage was sudden and accidental. The <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/tips/when-are-water-damage-and-mold-covered-by-insurance.html" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> says most homeowner and renter policies cover losses like a burst pipe, toilet overflow or broken washer hose. Policies usually exclude gradual leaks and seepage.
        </p>
        <p>
          Flooding is different. <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/" rel="noopener">FloodSmart</a>, the National Flood Insurance Program site, says most homeowners insurance does not cover flood damage, and a separate flood policy is needed. New flood policies normally take effect 30 days after purchase. For documentation tips and Texas claim deadlines, see these <a href="/water-damage-insurance-claim-tips/">water damage insurance claim tips</a>. Only your insurer can say what your policy covers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 10. DIY VS PRO -->
  <section class="section bg-light" id="diy-vs-pro">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head section-head--wide">
        <p class="eyebrow">Safety first</p>
        <h2>DIY Cleanup vs Calling a <em>Restoration Pro</em></h2>
        <p>Some first steps are safe for any homeowner. Others carry real risk from contamination, electricity or hidden water.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table data-table--two">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Safe for you to do</th><th scope="col">Time to call a licensed restoration pro</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Wipe up a small clean-water spill on a hard floor</td><td>Water soaked a large area, carpet and pad, or more than one room</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shut off the water at the fixture or main valve (see <a href="/burst-pipe-water-damage-baytown/">where to find the shutoff</a>)</td><td>Water came from a sewer line, a toilet with waste, or rising floodwater</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Move furniture, rugs, electronics and papers to a dry room</td><td>Water reached wall cavities, insulation or the ceiling</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Take photos and video for your insurance claim</td><td>Water is near outlets, cords or the breaker panel</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Run a dehumidifier and fans in a small, clean area</td><td>Mold is visible over a large area or the room smells musty</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Clean a small patch of mold under about 10 square feet, per EPA guidance</td><td>There was a fire, smoke or soot anywhere in the house</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="source-note">The 10 square foot guideline and the advice on contaminated water come from the <a href="https://www.epa.gov/mold/mold-cleanup-your-home" rel="noopener">EPA</a>. If a ceiling is sagging or the power may be wet, stay out and call for help.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 11. CHOOSING -->
  <section class="section" id="choosing">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="home-text home-text--center">
        <p class="eyebrow">Hiring</p>
        <h2>How to Choose a <em>Restoration Company</em> in Baytown</h2>
        <p class="lead">A good restoration company answers these plainly and in writing. Use this checklist before you sign.</p>
        <ul class="ticks ticks--single">
          <li><strong>Proof of insurance.</strong> Ask for a current certificate showing general liability and worker coverage.</li>
          <li><strong>A written estimate and scope.</strong> It should list what will be removed, what will be dried and how pricing works.</li>
          <li><strong>Moisture readings and drying logs.</strong> You should be able to see daily readings for every wet area.</li>
          <li><strong>Trained technicians.</strong> Ask whether the people coming out hold IICRC certificates such as Water Damage Restoration Technician.</li>
          <li><strong>A Texas mold license when needed.</strong> Mold remediation over 25 contiguous square feet generally requires a TDLR license. Look it up on the <a href="https://www.tdlr.texas.gov/LicenseSearch/" rel="noopener">TDLR license search</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>No storm-chaser red flags.</strong> Walk away from offers to waive your deductible, demands for a large upfront payment, or pressure to sign that day.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The Texas Department of Insurance says it is illegal for a contractor to waive your deductible or work it into the bid. The full checklist is in <a href="/choosing-a-restoration-company-baytown/">how to choose a restoration company in Baytown</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

